edb/ref 17592186702944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703155}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054499} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657686} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624849}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055945 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to **plot** the graph of $y = x + 2.$ We can do this by substituting some $x$-values into the equation, calculating the corresponding $y$-values, and plotting the $xy$-pairs. \n\nWe'll start by creating a table of values for $x$ and $y$, including a few values of $x$ to begin with.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing our equation $y=x+2,$ we can calculate the corresponding values for $y.$ So for $x=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\ny = -5+2 = {\\color{blue}{-3}},\n$$\n\nand we add this to our table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{-3}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe repeat this process to fill in the rest of the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/56/5677e939189394e8f98782bb5071730d4c916fe65857d673d887e0063d2ebce6.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a **straight line**.\n\n![](images/34/341d9dea9be40c6fd4065d20711f58c66b4a3cb4c667e3c100a08674d66041b3.png)\n\nThis is the graph of $y = x + 2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41e9e661-0eb1-5282-92e4-1d062834bd92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1436 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055946 :tutorial/content "The graph of a linear equation will *always* be a straight line! As we know, any straight line is defined by two points. \nTherefore, instead of plotting lots of points, we only need to plot two of them, and then we can draw a straight line through them. \n\nFor example, we previously encountered a line that passed through the points $(0,-2),$ $(1,1),$ $(2,4),$ and $(3,7).$ Its graph is shown below: \n\n![](images/d2/d2d8891102ce99020f544bd8d82a5ff0454080474ce4267ef5285db92f5b2ca0.png)\n\nHowever, given only two points $(0, -2)$ and $(3, 7)$, we can still draw the same line through them:\n\n![](images/65/6594282ddc3da3f96f2fb9983835776cd61e429188115361241a39f53b49d22d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "340a36d2-34fa-573b-b664-ce6c5bc161c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1437 :tutorial/title "A Line Passing Through Two Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059641 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059469} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3332b534-bd04-5e86-995e-1e6d691d8e1b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697090} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253571} {:db/id #