/ref 17592186639377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639378}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in an Expression Involving the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060876 :tutorial/content "The Pythagorean identity is\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to simplify trigonometric expressions. For example, let's try to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\theta \\left(\\sin \\theta + \\dfrac {\\cos^2 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe first expand the parentheses, and then simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n2(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta) =2\\cdot 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\theta \\left(\\sin \\theta + \\dfrac {\\cos^2 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\\right) = 2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "feab366d-3090-5d3c-80e0-82278d6e3ea3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2403 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
